  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nFall begins in a few hours, and cooler temperatures and brisk\nnorthwest winds give it that fall feeling. With showers\npersisting northwest of the Illinois River early this afternoon,\ntemperatures in that area have been confined to the upper 50s.\nSome breaks in the clouds over east central Illinois have\nallowed temperatures to reach near 70 in a few areas. Overall\nthe high-res models have not handled this rain especially well,\nthough the HRRR suggests it will persist for a few more hours.\n\nWater vapor imagery shows an upper low centered just southwest\nof Dubuque, and this low should tighten up a bit as it drops\nsoutheast into the Ohio Valley by late Wednesday morning.\nAnother slug of lower clouds will advect in from the east\nbeginning this evening within the circulation, persisting \nthrough much of Wednesday. Soundings show a fairly prominent dry\nlayer between 5-8kft, so any precipitation would probably be \nmore in the form of drizzle or sprinkles. Best chances would be\nin eastern Illinois, as soundings further west have a shallower\ndepth of moisture in the lower levels. With the persistent\nclouds, temperatures should be held back a bit, though some\nlower 70s can't be ruled out especially where some sunshine does\noccur.\n\nOverall, the longer range outlook looks rather tame, as a\ndeveloping nor'easter off the East Coast slows west-east\nprogression of any storm systems through much of the weekend.\nSprawling high pressure over southern Quebec, north of the\nstorm, will keep us in an easterly flow, keeping any \nsignificant warming trend at bay through the weekend. High \ntemperatures. It may take until early next week to fully \ndislodge this eastern low, at which we start getting more of a \nsoutherly wind flow. LREF probabilities of high temperatures\ngreater than 80 degrees range from 30-60% Monday and Tuesday\nfrom west central to southeast Illinois.",
